Hi Katherina,

Yes, it looks like event2dichot should preserve names and it doesn't. 
I've reported your bug to the sna package maintainer.

Here is an example.

 > n<-matrix(rnorm(25),nrow=5,ncol=5)
 > rownames(n)<-LETTERS[1:5]
 > colnames(n)<-LETTERS[1:5]
 > event2dichot(n,"mean")
      [,1] [,2] [,3] [,4] [,5]
[1,]    0    0    0    1    1
[2,]    1    0    1    0    0
[3,]    1    1    0    0    1
[4,]    1    1    1    1    1
[5,]    0    1    0    0    1


Fortunately, since you are using matrices, as a work around you should 
be able to do the conversion directly using a logical operator like ">":

n>mean(n)
       A     B     C     D     E
A FALSE FALSE FALSE  TRUE  TRUE
B  TRUE FALSE  TRUE FALSE FALSE
C  TRUE  TRUE FALSE FALSE  TRUE
D  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE
E FALSE  TRUE FALSE FALSE  TRUE

Or if you want to force R to convert the logicals into integers:

 > (n>0.5)+0
   A B C D E
A 0 0 0 1 1
B 0 0 1 0 0
C 0 0 0 0 1
D 1 1 1 1 0
E 0 0 0 0 1



On 04/09/2014 11:24 PM, Bohlecarbonell Katherina (ERD) wrote:
> Hello
>
> I have a longitudinal dataset with several small teams. The dependent matrix is valued (1-6). I'm dichotomizing (values greater than 3 are present) it for my analysis with rsiena by using event2dicho, but by doing it I have 2 challenges
>
> 1.)    Some datasets contain joiners/leavers which I identified with structural zero's (tie value = 10). This means that this tie would be shown as present even thou it may be absent as the actor has left the project.

OK, so you want a new matrix that includes only tie values > 3 and < 10 
, right?

# set up a test dataset
n<-matrix(round(runif(25,0,10)),nrow=5,dimnames=list(LETTERS[1:5],LETTERS[1:5]))
n
    A B  C D E
A  2 1 10 4 5
B  1 9  9 5 9
C 10 3  2 1 3
D  8 9  5 9 9
E  4 5 10 6 9

Should be able to just use logical operators:

(n>3 & n<10)+0
   A B C D E
A 0 0 0 1 1
B 0 1 1 1 1
C 0 0 0 0 0
D 1 1 1 1 1
E 1 1 0 1 1

Hopefully that works!


You may want to consider a data structure that allows you to represent 
vertex dynamics.  The networkDynamic package provides this, along with 
tools for maintaining dataset ids (check out the ?persistent.ids 
functions), but I don't know if it works directly RSiena.

best,
  -skye

