This is very helpful, thanks. Just to make this point clear, maybe the documentation for as.network could be amended as follows:

"as.network tries to coerce its argument to a network, using the network function if necessary. If the argument is already a network object, it is returned as-is and all other arguments are ignored."

I guess my next question is what to use for this conversion. I can always tell my students to use the igraph function for this, but doing a round-trip through a different package seems kind of clunky, especially for such a basic operation. I tried the following straightforward implementation:

as.undirected <- function(net)
{
  as.network(symmetrize(as.matrix(net), rule="weak"), directed=FALSE)
}

This gives me the right undirected structure, but loses vertex labels and attributes, which I hoped to be able to retain. (I can ignore edge attributes for my examples that call for directed-undirected conversion.) So my next try was

as.undirected <- function(net)
{
  undir = as.network(symmetrize(as.sociomatrix(net), rule="weak"), directed=FALSE)
  network.vertex.names(undir) = network.vertex.names(net)
  for (attr in list.vertex.attributes(net))
  {
    undir %v% attr <- net %v% attr
  }
  return(undir)
}

