-----Original Message-----
From: Carter T. Butts [mailto:buttsc@uci.edu] 
Sent: Friday, April 25, 2014 3:57 PM
To: statnet_help@u.washington.edu
Subject: Re: [statnet_help] Handling of matrices for undirected networks

Hi, Robin.  The reason that you are getting a directed adjacency matrix is that undir here is not undirected.  (You can verify that with
is.directed(undir).)  This is because you started out by creating a directed network object (net), and did not at any point symmetrize it; you seem to be trying to use as.network to do this, and that's not how that function works.  In particular, as.network will coerce its first argument to a network object, only invoking subsequent arguments if necessary to perform construction - if you pass it a network object, it will just return that object.  So this is not an error - that's what the function is supposed to do.

One could consider altering the functionality of as.network and friends to perform additional coercion, although this raises the usual issue that one would have to have a clear way of performing said coercion for all cases.  (This is not always trivial, particularly when you consider that network objects can be multiplex, carry complex attributes, etc.) If there's demand for that, it's something we might consider adding to future releases. In the interim, though, you should not assume that as.network and friends can be used to alter existing network objects.

Hope that helps,

-Carter

