Hi Michael,

There are two ways to address your error:

1. Implement structural zeros using the extreme value detection.
Basically you don't need to do anything and statnet will automatically
detect it and fix the coefficient to -Inf.  You will get a warning
message though.
Model_02 <- ergm(Y ~ edges + b1star(2) + edgecov(Z))

2. Fix the coefficient explicitly using offset.coef and turn off the
MPLE estimate of log likelihood using eval.loglik=FALSE. There could
be a bug in the last step of the estimate causing your error.
Model_03 <- ergm(Y ~ edges + b1star(2) + offset(edgecov(Z)) ,
offset.coef=-Inf, eval.loglik=FALSE )

The following toy models illustrate your error and the solutions. Let
me know if you cannot repeat it with your dataset.

Best,
Yun

mat <- mat.or.vec(3,4)
mat[1,] <- 1
mat[2,2] <- 1
Y <- as.network(mat, format=adjacency, bipartite = TRUE)
Z <- mat.or.vec(3,4)
Z[3,3] <- 1

Model_01 <- ergm(Y ~ edges + b1star(2) + offset(edgecov(Z)) , offset.coef=-Inf )

"Error in ergm(dind, estimate = "MPLE", constraints = constraints,
eval.loglik = FALSE,  :
  Invalid offset parameter vector offset.coef: wrong number of
parameters: expected 1 got 2."

Model_02 <- ergm(Y ~ edges + b1star(2) + edgecov(Z))

"Monte Carlo MLE Results:
          Estimate Std. Error MCMC % p-value
edges      -2.4278     1.1648      0  0.0668 .
b1star2     1.6675     0.7693      0  0.0583 .
edgecov.Z     -Inf         NA     NA      NA  "

Model_03 <- ergm(Y ~ edges + b1star(2) + offset(edgecov(Z)) ,
offset.coef=-Inf, eval.loglik=FALSE )

"Monte Carlo MLE Results:
          Estimate Std. Error MCMC % p-value
edges      -2.4356     1.1686      0  0.0668 .
b1star2     1.6746     0.7727      0  0.0584 .
edgecov.Z     -Inf         NA     NA      NA  "


