Hello,

I am trying to create a two-mode adjacency matrix. The data I have are structured such that the rows are events and the columns are the ID for persons at the event (e.g. for an event that had two people, the cell entry for the first column would be the ID of a person at the event and the cell entry for the second column would be the ID of the other person at the event). In a way, the data are structured like roster data, but for two-modes. I am trying to restructure the data so that the matrix is events X ID, so I can take the transpose to get the two-mode adjacency matrix.

Here is an example of the syntax I am using:

raw<-matrix(c("10","5",NA,"7","10","7",NA,"5","5","7"),nrow=5,ncol=2,byrow=T)

colnames(raw) <- c("ID of first person at event", "ID of second person at event")
rownames(raw) <-c("Event1","Event2","Event3","Event4","Event5")

ids     <-unique(as.vector(raw))
sid     <-sort(ids)
out     <-matrix(0,nrow=dim(raw)[1],ncol=length(sid))
id      <-match(ids,sid)
p1      <-match(raw[,1] ,sid)
p2      <-match(raw[,2] ,sid)

#Loop over the data to recode the ids into "1"
for (i in 1:nrow(raw)){
 if(!is.na(p1[i])) {out[id[i], p1[i]]<-1 }
 if(!is.na(p2[i])) {out[id[i], p2[i]]<-1 }
}
colnames(out) <- sid
rownames(out) <-c("Event1","Event2","Event3","Event4","Event5")

out # E4 and E5 do not show the correct data.

The problem appears to be with the length if the object "id". For some reason, it only goes through three rows (in this case). I cannot figure out how to get it to loop through the entire event list.

