Hi Skye,
Thanks for looking into this. Well, pmax() and pmin() are certainly nice
little functions!!!! and do exactly what I needed to do. I guess I did not
need to write all that code, though it was good R practice I suppose.

To answer your question about why I was using NAs, the reason is as
follows. I am using weak symmetrization, so if there is a tie reported by
only one ego in an ego - alter pair, I am saying they are connected. So I
was using NAs so that if I symmetrize by min, an ij-ji pair of 1-0 would
not get replaced with 0, but would get replace by 1 (obviously not an issue
with max). Than I replaced any NA-NA pairs at the end with 0. So valued
data is a tie, NA was just a place holder for intermediary steps, and 0 was
a non-tie. The final matrix has no missing ties and is just values 1 to 5
(ties), or 0 (non-tie).

I did find a work around to the problem I reported. I removed isolates in a
separate program before importing my data as a csv into R. I thus avoided
having to use the network object format and just worked with matrix
objects, so everything worked fine with the code I wrote. But, next time I
