From philip.leifeld at uni-konstanz.de  Tue Jul  8 07:44:44 2014
From: philip.leifeld at uni-konstanz.de (Philip Leifeld)
Date: Tue Jul  8 07:44:44 2014
Subject: [statnet_help] Bug in the network function when NAs are present in
	two-mode networks
Message-ID: <53BC03DC.1080705@uni-konstanz.de>

Dear Carter and others,

I think I have discovered a bug in the constructor for network objects. 
Two-mode (= non-square) matrices with NA values cause an error message 
when they are converted into bipartite network objects.

The reason seems to be that you are using the 'min' and 'max' functions 
without specifying 'na.rm = TRUE' several times in the 
'which.matrix.type' function. I will provide a minimal self-contained 
example as well as a proposed bugfix below.

It would be helpful if the SVN or Git repository was publicly 
accessible. Then users like me could propose bugfixes as pull requests.

Best,
Philip


Reproducible example:

##########
# one-mode network with NAs --> works fine!
mat <- matrix(rbinom(400, 1, 0.2), nrow = 20)
naIndices <- sample(1:400, 20)
mat[naIndices] <- NA
nw <- network(mat)

# two-mode network with NAs --> error!
mat <- matrix(rbinom(200, 1, 0.2), nrow = 20)
naIndices <- sample(1:200, 20)
mat[naIndices] <- NA
nw <- network(mat)
##########


Suggested bugfix:

1) Line 215 of file 'R/misc.R':

Change the following line:

else if (max(abs(x))==1 && max(abs(x-as.integer(x)))==0)

into:

else if (max(abs(x), na.rm = TRUE)==1 && max(abs(x-as.integer(x)), na.rm 
= TRUE)==0)

2) Line 217 of file 'R/misc.R':

Change the following line:

else if (max(abs(x-as.integer(x))[,1:2])==0 && min(x[,1:2])>0)

into:

else if (max(abs(x-as.integer(x))[,1:2], na.rm = TRUE)==0 && 
min(x[,1:2], na.rm = TRUE)>0)

