Subject: [statnet_help] Preserving the location of structural zeroes during
	simulations
Message-ID: <53BC3E45.9050400@uni-konstanz.de>

Dear statnet team,

It's me again, with another question. Sorry for flooding the mailing 
list with so many bug reports and questions today.

I would like to fit a model with structural zeroes and then simulate new 
networks from this model which preserve the structure of the zeroes. 
Let's say one fourth of all dyads are impossible to be realized. What I 
want is a number of simulated networks where exactly these dyads are not 
part of the simulation. Ultimately, I want to compare the simulations to 
the original network in a dyad-wise fashion, so I need to preserve the 
location of the structural zeroes.

I have found a constraint called "observed", but I don't know if this 
was designed to do what I want because the description of the 'observed' 
constraint in the documentation is fairly short. I have tried to replace 
the impossible values in the observed network by NAs, then told the ergm 
function additionally that these dyads are impossible to realize (via 
offsets), and finally told the simulate.ergm function to simulate values 
only where the original network was observed (= non-NA in my 
understanding). However, this does not seem to work because all dyadic 
values across all simulations are always the same values as in the 
observed network -- there is a perfect correlation between the observed 
network and all simulations although I was using only an 'edges' term. 
So I was wondering if the 'observed' constraint was actually supposed to 
do what I thought it was supposed to do. What else can I try?

Here is a self-contained example which demonstrates the behavior I have 
described above:

##########

library("statnet")
set.seed(12345)

# create 20 x 20 network with 100 NAs
mat <- matrix(rbinom(400, 1, 0.4), nrow = 20)
zeroIndices <- sample(1:400, 100)
diag(mat) <- 0
mat[zeroIndices] <- NA
nw <- network(mat)

# create matrix with structural zeroes at the same location as the NAs
structZero <- matrix(0, nrow = nrow(mat), ncol = ncol(mat))
structZero[zeroIndices] <- 1

# fit model with structural zeroes
model <- ergm(nw ~ edges + offset(edgecov(structZero)),
     offset.coef = -Inf)

# simulate 20 new networks from the model (only where nw is observed)
sim <- simulate.ergm(model, nsim = 20, constraints = ~ observed)

# pick one of the simulated networks and compare to original nw
s <- as.matrix(sim[[15]])
s[zeroIndices] <- NA
table(s == mat)  # perfect correlation?!

##########

I would be happy if somebody could provide a hint (a) on what the 
'observed' constraint is supposed to do, and (b) how to preserve 
structural zeroes when simulating new networks.

Thanks in advance and best regards

Philip

