From: buttsc at uci.edu (Carter T. Butts)
Date: Tue Jul  8 16:43:11 2014
Subject: [statnet_help] Netlogit on ego-network
In-Reply-To: <CAG9He6+asf+oQOzcqAWfoFZ6a+BrsY0gejLa-WV-2SzKUc8MjA@mail.gmail.com>
References: <CAG9He6+asf+oQOzcqAWfoFZ6a+BrsY0gejLa-WV-2SzKUc8MjA@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <53BC820F.5090205@uci.edu>

Hi, Vanesse-

On 07/08/2014 01:35 AM, vanesse labeyrie wrote:
> Hi,
> I want to peform network logistic regression on an ego-network :
> I ran it on a rectangular matrix with the ego in lines and alter in 
> columns.
> Is it the right way to do it ?
>

Since the ego net is conditional on ego having an edge to each alter 
(and since you are not also including those to whom ego is not tied), 
including ego/alter dyads in an egocentric netlogit analysis will 
produce biased results.  If you wanted to analyze an ego net in this 
way, you would need to remove ego's row and column from the adjacency 
matrix and consider only alter-alter edge variables. (Whether this is a 
good idea depends on what you are trying to accomplish, and what you are 
willing to assume.)

> I have a high proportion of 0 in the matrix - does this may act 
> wrongly on the performance of LR-QAP ?
>

No.  But be aware that QAP is not very robust in the nonlinear case (of 
which logistic regression is an example) when you have multiple 
covariates.  I would also be surprised if you could get much information 
out of a regression on a single ego net, unless your mean degree is >20 
or so.

Hope that helps,

-Carter

