Hi,

I'm trying to use ERGM on an egocentric network, but I'm not used at all to
this approach and would need some help :

I interviewed 187 people (ego) and asked them to cite all the people with
who they exchanged goods in their life.  So *they cited mainly people who
were not interviewed* (561 people cited =alter).
I want to test the effect of ethnic membership on this exchange network.

Following the tutorial on ERGM for egocentric data, I tried to model a
network of size N=187, based on the ethnic mixing matrix (divided by 2
here) :

> ego.mixmat


         E1   E2  E3
Ethny 1  119  13  68.5
Ethny 2  8    70  10.5
Ethny 3  49   9   86.0


ego.edges <- sum(ego.mixmat)
ego.ethnymatch <- ego.mixmat[1,1]+ego.mixmat[2,2] +ego.mixmat[3,3]
ego.target.stats <- c(ego.edges, ego.ethnymatch)
ego.fit <- ergm(ego.net ~ edges + nodematch('ethny'), target.stats =
ego.target.stats)

Is it the right way to do ?

I found my degree distribution quite difficult to model :


For instance, for degree 2 and 3 the simulations are well bellow observed
values, but it is also the case for degree 5, 6, 9 ... shall I include
terms for each of these degree in my model ?  It makes a lot of terms !



Many thanks for your answers and help,

Vanesse



2014-07-09 1:43 GMT+02:00 Carter T. Butts <buttsc@uci.edu>:

>  Hi, Vanesse-
>
>
> On 07/08/2014 01:35 AM, vanesse labeyrie wrote:
>
>    Hi,
>  I want to peform network logistic regression on an ego-network :
>  I ran it on a rectangular matrix with the ego in lines and alter in
> columns.
>  Is it the right way to do it ?
>
>
> Since the ego net is conditional on ego having an edge to each alter (and
> since you are not also including those to whom ego is not tied), including
> ego/alter dyads in an egocentric netlogit analysis will produce biased
> results.  If you wanted to analyze an ego net in this way, you would need
> to remove ego's row and column from the adjacency matrix and consider only
> alter-alter edge variables.  (Whether this is a good idea depends on what
> you are trying to accomplish, and what you are willing to assume.)
>
>
>  I have a high proportion of 0 in the matrix - does this may act wrongly
> on the performance of LR-QAP ?
>
>
> No.  But be aware that QAP is not very robust in the nonlinear case (of
> which logistic regression is an example) when you have multiple
> covariates.  I would also be surprised if you could get much information
> out of a regression on a single ego net, unless your mean degree is >20 or
> so.
>
> Hope that helps,
>
> -Carter
