Hey all.

Thank you for your recent responses regarding ego-net ERGM examples and triangle terms. I appreciate Jeffrey Smith's (2012) Soc Meth article. It indeed gives me the blueprint I was looking for, but is seemingly overly complicated. I wonder if someone can help me clear up a detail. Hopefully I've understood the article well.

The objective is to fit an ERGM (including a GWESP term) to egocentric data.

Smith builds an algorithm to optimize the model fit using 1) ERGM constraints for degree distribution, 2) a conditional logit for mixing/homophily terms, and 3) chi-square as a distance measure for the ego-net configuration distribution, where chi-square is minimized by adjusting GWESP parameter(s). Optimization is achieved in an iterative fashion using these tools.

I think the value is in item 3. I cannot figure out why Smith uses the conditional logit in item 2. Could anyone comment on an alternative strategy below?

Specifically, since degree distribution and mixing can be estimated unbiasedly from ego-net data, why couldn't parameters for degree and mixing terms be fit using the optimization routines included in statnet's ergm functionality? Perhaps then one could fix GWESP parameters at a variety of locations, each time optimizing degree and mixing term parameters (ideally in an automated way), to minimze the chi-square distance measure, and thus optimize the GWESP parameter.

It's still a bit kludgy,  but it seems less so than Smith's algorithm. Is my thinking flawed? I appreciate any comments. Thank you.

-Nate

--
Nathan J. Doogan, Ph.D.  | College of Public Health
Post-Doctoral Researcher | The Ohio State University

