3. Finally, something weird appears to be happening the larger the model gets (and in fact, particularly when the model gets right up against the limit I set in MPLE.max.dyad.types, which I believe here I increased to 1e+7). In the first model, everything seems to be running normally. In the second model, I add an additional set of terms (namely, nodefactor terms related to income); and while the parameter estimates still seem reasonable, all of a sudden the standard errors increase tenfold, almost nothing is significant, and the AIC and BIC skyrocket. (Note, however, that this is not something weird with the income terms- the same thing happens in the third model, when I try religion terms instead of income.) Again, all of this is going on in the territory when the model is about as big as it could possibly get- adding many more terms means it exceeds the max.dyad.types limit- and this seems to be the only time when I run into this particular kind of problem.

